 lines, we could allocate a vertical line for column n-2, which uses one non-sunny line. That seems efficient. Could we use a sunny line to cover one of these points? Yes, a sunny line can intersect column n-2 at exactly one point. So we could cover one point with a sunny line, and the other two with a vertical line? Actually a vertical line covers all three, so we wouldn't need a sunny line there. But we could also use a vertical line for column n-2, and use both sunny lines elsewhere. That seems plausible.\n\nThus we can assume we have a vertical line x=n-2 among the non-sunny lines. The solution says \"forcing a vertical line\". That is plausible.\n\nNow consider column n-1 (x=n-1) has points (n-1,1) and (n-1,2). Column n (x=n) has point (n,1). We need to cover these three points. We have remaining lines: n-2 non-sunny lines total, one used for x=n-2, leaving n-3 non-sunny lines. Also we have 2 sunny lines. So we have n-1 lines left to cover columns n-1 and n and all other columns.\n\nNow, the solution says \"point (n,1) cannot be covered by a sunny line together with points in column n-1 (any such line is horizontal or slope -1, both non-sunny).\" Let's examine that claim. Suppose we try to cover (n,1) with a sunny line. Since a sunny line is not vertical, it will intersect column n-1 at some point (if it passes through column n-1). But the line passing through (n,1) and (n-1,1) is horizontal (y=1), which is non-sunny. The line passing through (n,1) and (n-1,2) has slope (2-1)/(n-1 - n) = 1/(-1) = -1, which is slope -1, also non-sunny. So any line through (n,1) and a point in column n-1 will be either horizontal (if the point is (n-1,1)) or slope -1 (if the point is (n-1,2)). Both are non-sunny. So a sunny line cannot pass through (n,1) and any point in column n-1. So if we want to cover (n,1) with a sunny line, that line cannot also cover any point in column n-1. So we would need to cover column n-1 points with other lines (non-sunny or sunny). But we have only