orizontal lines, D = number of slope -1 lines. So V+H+D = n-2.\n\nEach vertical line covers all points in a column. Each horizontal line covers at most one point per column (since each column has at most one point at a given y). Each slope -1 line covers at most one point per column as well (since each column has at most one point with a given sum x+y). So vertical lines are the only non-sunny lines that can cover more than one point in a column. So to cover column n-2's 3 points, we need either a vertical line, or at least 3 non-vertical lines (horizontal or slope -1) dedicated to that column. If we use a vertical line, we use 1 non-sunny line to cover 3 points. If we don't, we need at least 3 non-vertical lines for that column. Let's see if we can afford that.\n\nIf we use a vertical line for column n-2, we use 1 non-sunny line. Then we have n-3 non-sunny lines left to cover the remaining columns (including columns n-1 and n). If we don't use a vertical line for column n-2, we need at least 3 non-vertical lines for that column, leaving at most n-5 non-sunny lines for the rest. That seems even more restrictive. So using a vertical line is optimal. So it's plausible that we can assume a vertical line for column n-2 without loss of generality. So the claim is acceptable.\n\nNow, after using vertical line x=n-2, we have remaining columns n-1 and n. The solution says point (n,1) cannot be covered by a sunny line together with points in column n-1 (any such line is horizontal or slope -1, both non-sunny). This forces a third sunny line, contradicting k=2.\n\nLet's examine that claim. We have exactly 2 sunny lines. Suppose we try to cover column n-1 and n with the remaining lines. We have used one vertical line for column n-2. We have n-2 non-sunny lines total, so we have n-3 non-sunny lines left. We have 2 sunny lines left. So total lines left = n-1 lines (n-3 non-sunny + 2 sunny). We need to cover columns n-1 and n and all other columns (1..n-3). But we can use vertica