e can cover at most k points. So total points covered by k sunny lines is at most k^2. The total points needed is k(k+1)/2. Since k^2 >= k(k+1)/2 for all k >= 1, this is not a restriction. So we need a stronger argument.\n\nMaybe the column counting argument is that each sunny line can intersect each column at most once, so across n columns, each sunny line can cover at most n points. So total points covered by sunny lines is at most kn. But we have many points: total points = n(n+1)/2. The vertical lines cover all points in n-k columns, each column has at most n points? Actually column i has at most n+1-i points. So total points covered by vertical lines = sum_{i=1}^{n-k} (n+1-i) = (n+1)*(n-k) - (n-k)(n-k+1)/2 = (n+1)(n-k) - (n-k)(n-k+1)/2. The remaining points to be covered by sunny lines = total points - points covered by vertical lines. Let's compute: total points = n(n+1)/2. Subtract vertical coverage: (n+1)(n-k) - (n-k)(n-k+1)/2 = (n+1)(n-k) - ((n-k)(n-k+1))/2. Let's compute difference: n(n+1)/2 - [(n+1)(n-k) - (n-k)(n-k+1)/2] = n(n+1)/2 - (n+1)(n-k) + (n-k)(n-k+1)/2 = [n(n+1) - 2(n+1)(n-k) + (n-k)(n-k+1)]/2 = [n(n+1) - 2(n+1)(n-k) + (n-k)(n-k+1)]/2.\n\nSimplify: n(n+1) = (n+1)n. 2(n+1)(n-k) = 2(n+1)n - 2(n+1)k. So n(n+1) - 2(n+1)(n-k) = n(n+1) - 2(n+1)n + 2(n+1)k = -n(n+1) + 2(n+1)k = (n+1)(2k - n). Then add (n-k)(n-k+1). So numerator = (n+1)(2k - n) + (n-k)(n-k+1). So remaining points = [(n+1)(2k - n) + (n-k)(n-k+1)]/2.\n\nWe need to cover these remaining points with k sunny lines. Each sunny line can intersect each column at most once, but the remaining points are in the rightmost k columns. So each sunny line can cover at most k points (one per column). So total capacity of sunny lines = k*k = k^2. So we need k^2 >= remaining points. So we need k^2 >= [(n+1)(2k - n) + (n-k)(n-k+1)]/2.\n\nWe can try to see for which k this holds for all n≥3. For k large relative to n, the term (2k - n) may be positive. Let's test k = n (i.e., all lines sunny). Then remainin